--TEST--
A walk refuses everything `Core\Xml::parse` refuses, but where the walk reaches it: the nodes before the refusal were answered, and the reader does not advance past what it refused — `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`, `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Xml\Reader::depth
// The stream is the same strict parser as the tree, read one node at a time, so
// the refusals are the same sentences. What differs is *when*: a tree is all or
// nothing, and a walk has already answered every node before the one it
// refuses. Neither repairs anything.
class Walk {
    public static function upTo(Core\Xml\Reader $reader): void {
        try {
            while (true) {
                ?Core\Xml\Node $node = $reader->read();
                if ($node != null) {
                    echo "  node \"", $node->name(), $node->text(), "\"\n";
                } else {
                    echo "  end\n";
                    break;
                }
            }
        } catch (ParseError $malformed) {
            echo "  refused: ", $malformed->message, "\n";
        }
    }
}

// A second root element: the first one and everything in it are already the
// program's, and the refusal arrives at the tag that cannot be there.
echo "a second root:\n";
Core\Xml\Reader $second = Core\Xml::reader("<order><line/></order><invoice/>");
Walk::upTo($second);

// The walk does not advance past what it refused, so asking again reports the
// same sentence rather than reading on from the other side of it. A refused
// document stays refused.
try {
    $second->read();
    echo "  the walk read past its own refusal\n";
} catch (ParseError $again) {
    echo "  asked again: ", $again->message, "\n";
}

// A refusal writes nothing, and the depth is part of that: it still belongs to
// the last node the walk answered rather than to the tag it refused.
echo "  depth after the refusal: ", $second->depth(), "\n";

// An element closes as what it opened as, and the walk knows what is open
// because that is the one thing it keeps.
echo "a mismatched closing tag:\n";
Walk::upTo(Core\Xml::reader("<a><b>t</a>"));

// The end of a document with something still open is not the end of a
// document.
echo "an element left open:\n";
Walk::upTo(Core\Xml::reader("<a><b>t"));

// The refusals a walk makes before it answers anything at all: this class's
// answer to XXE is that no code path resolves an entity, so a document type
// declaration is refused whole rather than read with fetching turned off.
echo "a document type declaration:\n";
Walk::upTo(Core\Xml::reader("<!DOCTYPE order SYSTEM \"http://example.invalid/o.dtd\"><order/>"));

echo "an entity that names nothing:\n";
Walk::upTo(Core\Xml::reader("<a>&xxe;</a>"));
--EXPECT--
a second root:
  node "order"
  node "line"
  refused: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): a document has one root element, and this is a second one.
  asked again: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): a document has one root element, and this is a second one.
  depth after the refusal: 1
a mismatched closing tag:
  node "a"
  node "b"
  node "t"
  refused: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): `<b>` is closed by `</a>`, and an element closes as what it opened as.
an element left open:
  node "a"
  node "b"
  node "t"
  refused: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): `<b>` is never closed.
a document type declaration:
  refused: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): a document type declaration is not read at all — no entity is resolved from an external subset, from an internal subset or from the network, so there is nothing a `<!DOCTYPE …>` could mean here.
an entity that names nothing:
  node "a"
  refused: Core\Xml\Reader::read(): `&xxe;` is not one of the five predefined entities, and no entity is resolved from a document type declaration, an internal subset or the network.
